WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.040
It seems like strange horror movie cameos are&nbsp;
about as integral to the genre as jump scares&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:10.560
and creepy kids. From major stars playing unusual&nbsp;
roles to non-Hollywood celebrities lending their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:16.240
star power to screamfests, here's a fond look back&nbsp;
at the weirdest horror movie cameos of all time.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:21.360
If you've ever thought to yourself that Fabio,&nbsp;
with his heavenly eyes and flowing hair, looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:27.120
like an angel, you're not alone. The filmmakers&nbsp;
behind 1990's The Exorcist III apparently thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:32.240
so too. They cast the young heartthrob in his&nbsp;
first U.S. role as an angel. Another bizarre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:38.080
cameo in the film comes courtesy of NBA star&nbsp;
Patrick Ewing, who appears as the Angel of Death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:43.280
Both characters show up onscreen during a surreal&nbsp;
dream sequence that features an angel leading a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:48.880
big band, a train station that seems to be heaven,&nbsp;
and other random acts of chaos and madness. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:53.520
that's not all, The Exorcist III has plenty&nbsp;
of other odd celebrity cameos, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.520 --> 00:01:00.320
Samuel L. Jackson, Larry King and, perhaps most&nbsp;
strangely, former Surgeon General C. Everett Koop.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:05.520
Despite its unique cameos and terrifying&nbsp;
premise, the film received a lukewarm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:09.840
response from critics. It seems like when&nbsp;
you're making a second sequel to arguably the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:14.880
best horror movie ever made, you have to make&nbsp;
it stranger if you can't make it any scarier.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:20.160
These days, Bill Murray's public demeanor seems to be closer to his classic characters

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:26.000
in Ghostbusters and Caddyshack than the persona&nbsp;
of most A-list actors. This is why it was such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.760
a delight to see the legendary&nbsp;
comedian appear in Zombieland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:32.800
playing a slightly exaggerated version of himself.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:34.640
"Wait, who's Bill Murray?"

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:40.560
"Hey, I've never hit a kid before. I&nbsp;
mean, that's like asking who Gandhi is."

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:47.040
While Zombieland is a horror comedy, Murray's&nbsp;
cameo completely changes the tone of the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:52.480
from slightly scary to nearly pure comedy as&nbsp;
the four main characters wander into Murray's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:58.480
seemingly abandoned mansion. The set design&nbsp;
is pure Murray myth, featuring props from his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:02.560
previous movies and ludicrous paintings&nbsp;
of the man hanging throughout the house.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:08.080
It's the perfect foreshadowing for when&nbsp;
Murray, himself, eventually pops up. And he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:13.440
in surprisingly good spirits for someone who's&nbsp;
survived the zombie apocalypse for so long. Murray&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:19.120
ends up having a blast with the protagonists&nbsp;
right up until a prank goes wrong in an extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:25.840
gruesome way. It's by far the comedic centerpiece&nbsp;
of a very funny movie, and Zombieland wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:31.600
be the same without the surprise of seeing the&nbsp;
beloved actor delivering some truly classic lines.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:35.840
"So, do you have any regrets?"

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:38.400
"Garfield, maybe."

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:44.400
Silence of the Lambs is one of the greatest&nbsp;
horror movies ever made, and also one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:49.760
few to ever earn an Academy Award. Many&nbsp;
factors led to that critical acclaim: the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:55.200
steady direction of Jonathan Demme, the nuanced&nbsp;
performance of Jodie Foster, and, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:01.840
the scenery-chewing, flesh-eating character of&nbsp;
Hannibal Lecter, played by Anthony Hopkins. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:07.280
an incredibly engrossing movie, but if you're&nbsp;
a mega-fan of crooning songwriter Chris Isaak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:11.040
you might get pulled out of the movie&nbsp;
when he suddenly shows up on screen.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.840
Isaak, the musician behind the hit song "Wicked&nbsp;
Game," appears towards the end of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.880
as a SWAT commander during&nbsp;
Lecter's breakout scene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:23.760
It's an incredibly tense and climactic&nbsp;
moment in the film that unfortunately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:29.360
might be lessened for you if you spend most of&nbsp;
the scene going, "Wait, was that Chris Isaak?"

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:32.080
Of course, Isaak has appeared&nbsp;
in other films since,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:36.480
most notably in David Lynch's Twin Peaks:&nbsp;
Fire Walk With Me, but his cameo in Silence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:40.800
of the Lambs is definitely one of&nbsp;
his most surprising appearances.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:46.080
While some viewers might find it jarring,&nbsp;
plenty of movie fans had another reaction.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:46.880
"That was good."

00:03:49.478 --> 00:03:53.120
The Backstreet Boys warned us all along that&nbsp;
they'd be back, but we still didn't believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:58.560
them. Or at least we didn't think that they'd be&nbsp;
back in the closing minutes of This Is the End.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:03.520
In this star-studded horror-comedy, most of the&nbsp;
action takes place around James Franco's house&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:08.640
as real-life celebrities are menaced by&nbsp;
supernatural events and insane survivors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:14.080
of the rapture. Perhaps unsurprisingly, the&nbsp;
rapture doesn't include a decent chunk of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:18.800
the Hollywood elite, leaving Jonah Hill,&nbsp;
Seth Rogen, James Franco, Danny McBride,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:22.800
Craig Robinson, and Jay Baruchel&nbsp;
to hole up and hide from demons.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:28.080
Eventually, Baruchel and Rogen are able to ascend&nbsp;
to heaven, where they can have everything they've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:34.000
ever dreamed of just by wishing for it.&nbsp;
Baruchel, in a truly relatable moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:38.240
wishes for the Backstreet Boys to show&nbsp;
up and perform one of their biggest hits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:42.480
They say that horror and comedy both rely&nbsp;
on surprise, so it makes sense that a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:47.200
horror comedy would end with one of the most&nbsp;
surprising and danceable cameos of all time.

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:53.120
Sometimes it's not the actual presence of a&nbsp;
celebrity in a horror movie that's surprising,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:58.640
but rather how they use that celebrity. Instead&nbsp;
of getting wrapped up in the ambiance of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:03.360
you end up scratching your head wondering what&nbsp;
the purpose of such a bizarre cameo even was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:10.240
A perfect example is Tim Burton's 1999&nbsp;
adaptation of Sleepy Hollow, which cast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:16.000
Christopher Walken as the Headless Horseman.&nbsp;
Let's be specific here, however: Walken plays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:21.440
the Headless Horseman before he loses his&nbsp;
head. In other words, he plays a Horseman.

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:25.680
Wordplay aside, Walken's character doesn't&nbsp;
utter a single line of dialogue during his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:30.640
brief appearance in the film. Instead, he&nbsp;
growls and hisses while fighting and trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:37.680
to escape capture. Considering the unique joy of&nbsp;
Walken's voice, it's completely insane to cast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:42.880
the acclaimed actor in a dialogue-free&nbsp;
role where he only snarls and grunts.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:48.160
You almost certainly know Tommy&nbsp;
Wiseau for his cult hit film The Room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:51.840
in which he delivers a truly&nbsp;
inimitable, bizarre performance.

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:54.800
"You're tearing me apart, Lisa!"

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:58.640
Wiseau has the kind of strange charisma where,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:03.600
no matter what, you're genuinely distracted&nbsp;
when he appears on screen, which was bad news&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:09.680
for 2016's Cold Moon. Despite Wiseau's cameo and&nbsp;
a story by the writer of Beetlejuice, Cold Moon is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:15.280
definitely not a comedy. In fact, it's deathly&nbsp;
serious, which makes his cameo even stranger.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:19.440
In the horror drama, Wiseau appears&nbsp;
for just a few moments with a snake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:22.560
and he performs as if he's never&nbsp;
seen one before in his life.

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:27.520
"Don't stretch him too much. You're&nbsp;
tearing him apart, for god's sake!"

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:31.360
There's almost nothing more&nbsp;
surprising than seeing Wiseau in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:37.040
otherwise straightforward dramatic horror movie,&nbsp;
especially when his presence bends the movie like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:41.360
gravity. Having him appear in the first&nbsp;
10 minutes of the film just means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:45.520
the audience is going to spend the next 80&nbsp;
minutes wondering if he's ever coming back.

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:48.480
"Ha, ha. You must be kidding, aren't you?"

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:54.720
Technically, this is less of a cameo and more of&nbsp;
a small role, but Jack Black's performance as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:58.880
overly familiar weed dealer named Titus&nbsp;
in I Still Know What You Did Last Summer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:03.600
is weird enough to merit inclusion. At the time&nbsp;
of the film's release, Black hadn't yet reached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:08.160
the heights of fame he'd scale after his work&nbsp;
in School of Rock and Kung Fu Panda, but he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:12.960
still recognizable for his role in the Tenacious&nbsp;
D television show and other small movie parts.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:16.800
The sequel to I Know What You Did Last&nbsp;
Summer takes the surviving characters to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:20.800
the Bahamas for a vacation where they&nbsp;
once again have to fend off the evil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:25.600
advances of the maniac from the first movie.&nbsp;
Black's Titus is one of the killer's first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:30.880
victims as he's stabbed with a hook and then&nbsp;
impaled with garden shears. To his credit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:36.400
Black totally commits to the part, giving Titus&nbsp;
a lot of presence on screen. Although, at times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:43.840
it feels as if Black is behaving&nbsp;
like he would on any given day.

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:48.400
The original Scream more or less&nbsp;
reinvented the horror genre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:53.120
pushing mainstream horror out of the cookie&nbsp;
cutter rut that it had been stuck in for so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:57.360
long and giving audiences characters that&nbsp;
had at least seen a horror movie before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:00.800
even if that knowledge didn't keep&nbsp;
them from falling prey to a killer.

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:06.080
As with most successful horror films, Scream&nbsp;
created its own franchise, which led to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:12.560
well-received Scream 2, but then started to peter&nbsp;
out with Scream 3. Still the second sequel does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:18.080
offer one delightful surprise cameo: Carrie&nbsp;
Fisher playing an annoyed studio secretary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:24.000
named Bianca who bears an uncanny resemblance to&nbsp;
Carrie Fisher and was almost cast in Star Wars.

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:28.000
"I was up for Princess Leia. I was this close.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:31.200
So, who gets it? The one who&nbsp;
sleeps with George Lucas."

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:36.240
Fisher's cameo is a welcome surprise&nbsp;
in an otherwise pretty lackluster film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:40.240
And there's plenty to enjoy in Fisher&nbsp;
playing a character that's mistaken for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:44.720
Fisher in a movie franchise that jumps&nbsp;
between movie fiction and movie reality.

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:51.360
From start to finish, The Cabin in the Woods seems&nbsp;
to be obsessed with cataloguing and referencing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:57.120
the formula of horror movies. Nearly every&nbsp;
scene in the film either directly references or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:03.040
obliquely nods to popular horror conventions&nbsp;
or even specific horror flicks. That extends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:08.560
all the way to the climax, in which Dana and Marty&nbsp;
find out that they're all part of a grand ongoing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:13.920
sacrifice which requires certain archetypes&nbsp;
to be ritualistically killed for the pleasure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:20.560
of elder gods. Marty needs to die since he's the&nbsp;
"fool," while the "virgin" can survive without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:25.920
ruining the ceremony. It's a pretty clear nod&nbsp;
to the Final Girl convention, a term coined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:32.080
by writer Carol Clover in her book Men, Women,&nbsp;
and Chainsaws: Gender in the Modern Horror Film.

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:36.400
Naturally, the director of the institute in&nbsp;
charge of sacrificing people for elder gods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:40.560
is a "Final Girl" in her own right,&nbsp;
someone who survived a killer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:46.000
Except, you might have expected the part to be&nbsp;
played by Jamie Lee Curtis, one of cinema's most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:51.200
notable scream queens from classic slasher films&nbsp;
like Halloween. The character is instead played by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:55.200
Sigourney Weaver. It's a really surprising&nbsp;
cameo, mostly because Weaver's role in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.200 --> 00:10:00.320
Alien franchise doesn't exactly line up with&nbsp;
Cabin in the Woods' central statement about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:05.680
formulaic horror conventions. Curtis' place in&nbsp;
the popular horror consciousness would have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:11.440
a much more obvious choice, while Weaver's cameo&nbsp;
almost undercuts the central point of the film.

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:16.320
Sometimes, breaking the fourth wall in&nbsp;
a movie works like a charm and adds an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:21.680
extra dimension to the narrative, but when said&nbsp;
wall is unexpectedly smashed to pieces by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:27.200
professional wrestler who has nothing to do with&nbsp;
the rest of the film, it can be a little jarring.

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:32.480
The 1984 horror-comedy Gremlins gets pretty&nbsp;
weird in some places and the 1990 sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:36.960
Gremlins 2: The New Batch, takes&nbsp;
the craziness to another level.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:41.280
Joe Dante's satire of Hollywood sequels&nbsp;
is full of Easter eggs and cameos, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:48.080
the most memorable and bizarre comes by&nbsp;
way of former WWE superstar, Hulk Hogan.

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:53.120
When the little terrors commandeer a movie&nbsp;
theater that is somehow showing Gremlins 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:56.720
the manager of the theater seeks help&nbsp;
from Hogan, who happens to be in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.720 --> 00:11:01.120
audience. After getting worked up about&nbsp;
the film being interrupted by gremlins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:05.840
the Hulkster addresses the annoying green&nbsp;
creatures before he pile-drives the fourth wall...

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:13.840
"If I were you, I'd run the rest of Gremlins 2,&nbsp;
right now. Sorry, folks, it won't happen again."

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:22.240
There are so many weird celebrity cameos in&nbsp;
Sharknado 3: Oh Hell No! that you'd be forgiven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.240 --> 00:11:28.640
for not spotting them all upon your first viewing.&nbsp;
Shahs of Sunset cast member Reza Farahan plays a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:33.600
Capitol Park policeman. Former Malcolm in the&nbsp;
Middle star Frankie Muniz appears as a mechanic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:39.040
George R.R. Martin can be spotted as a moviegoer.&nbsp;
Even Penn and Teller have a short scene in a diner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:45.280
with David Hasselhoff. The list goes on and&nbsp;
on, but the one celeb cameo that seemed to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:51.920
everyone talking after SyFy dropped the B-movie&nbsp;
sequel in 2015 involved TV's Jerry Springer.

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:58.400
In Sharknado 3: Oh Hell No!, the East Coast has&nbsp;
been rechristened the "Feast Coast" by the press.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:03.600
This is because there's a massive sharknado headed&nbsp;
in that direction. Knowing that his wife, April,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:08.960
and their family are spending the day at Universal&nbsp;
Orlando, veteran sharknado hunter Fin Shepard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:15.200
heads for Florida. Along the way, he's joined by&nbsp;
Nova, an expert sharknado tracker. Upon arrival,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:19.520
the pair walk straight into a nightmare the&nbsp;
famous theme park is plunged into chaos when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:24.560
the sharknado finally hits. In the middle of&nbsp;
the mayhem, Springer's character really wants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:30.320
his picture taken next to a replica of the shark&nbsp;
from Jaws. Unfortunately for him, it's not a prop.

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:38.055
"Please, please, take my picture. Real fast!"

00:12:38.055 --> 00:12:39.074
"There's no time for this.&nbsp;
You have to get out of here."

00:12:39.074 --> 00:12:39.360
"Nova, come on!"

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:41.520
Check out one of our newest&nbsp;
videos right here! Plus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:45.120
even more Looper videos about your&nbsp;
favorite horror movies are coming soon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:51.840
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit&nbsp;
the bell so you don't miss a single one.

